ent on the value of university  alternatives such as apprenticeships  and because over the last few years you  now quite often  see very prestigious and secondary  schools as  you know very expensive ones they will  announce not only are they got x many  kids to you know bristol to durham to  oxford to cambridge  but they might also say by the way we've  got two students on an apprenticeship  for jaguar land rover or something like  that they seem to be as proud as that of  that  as they are of getting into the  universities in the old days so  it seems that apprenticeships are being  certainly seen  to be as glittering a prize as a place  as some of the best universities yeah i  mean i think an apprenticeship at jaguar  land rover is a fantastic thing to do  so good for great for people who get it  the the issue i don't think is the  prestige  of apprenticeships it's when you think  of apprenticeships like that  the supply is quite limited an  apprenticeship is an actual employer  wishing to employ a young person then  invest in their education and training  and it's and i think it's no accident  you think of  of uh convention not conventional but  established manufacturing firms we  that's the kind of environment where we  think of the apprenticeship  they're great but they just aren't going  to get the volume  that is required unless we massively  changed our labor market  it's no accident that high education  participation is particularly high  in liberal market economies with  relatively unregulated labor markets  if you go down the german route with  many more protected activities  many more licenses to practice that you  need to secure  through an apprenticeship and you can't  do this job unless you've done an  apprenticeship and reach the following  requirement  you can't call yourself an electrician  or whatever then  you you reverse a lot of our labor  market changes  and you can create an environment where  there are many more protected jobs and  apprenti